PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The ESA requires employers with 25+ employees (as counted on January 1 of the relevant year) to maintain written policies on both disconnecting from work and electronic monitoring. Any such policy must be given to every employee within 30 days of its creation or any update; new hires must also receive it within 30 days.  

Option C meets the head-count test (≈1,000 employees) and therefore must satisfy the 30-day distribution rule. Updating monthly but distributing only quarterly necessarily means some updates are not provided inside the statutory 30-day window, producing clear non-compliance as of 2 January 2023.  

All other options either fall below the 25-employee threshold on 1 January 2023 (so no policy obligation exists) or, where a policy is required, the facts supplied do not indicate any missed distribution deadline. Hence C is the only employer out of compliance.}

5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The ESA requires employers with 25+ employees (counted on January 1 of a given year) to maintain written policies on both disconnecting from work and electronic monitoring. When a policy is created or revised, a copy must be given to every employee within 30 days (and to new hires within 30 days of hire).  

- A, D and E all have fewer than 25 employees on the relevant January 1 date, so neither policy is required.  
- B has only 23 employees, so the policies are voluntary and distribution rules do not apply.  
- C has ~1,000 employees, therefore both policies are mandatory; however, monthly revisions followed by quarterly distribution routinely exceed the 30-day deadline, placing the employer out of compliance as of 2 January 2023.  

Thus C is the only option that violates the statute on the date in question.}

